{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01YW3osq6rq1DKfQkwJfiCz9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The pattern is:\n- `0` → `\"0\"`\n- `512` → `\"512\"` (no suffix, integer format)\n- `1024` → `\"1.0K\"` (has suffix, always 1 decimal even if whole)\n- `1536` → `\"1.5K\"` (has suffix, 1 decimal)\n- `10240` → `\"10K\"` (has suffix, but `10.0` → `\"10K\"` not `\"10.0K\"`)\n\nWait, `10240` → `\"10K\"`. 10240/1024 = 10.0 exactly. \n\nSo the rule seems to be: format with `%.1f`, then strip trailing `.0` only when the integer part is >= 10? No...\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the examples: `1.0K` keeps the decimal, but `10K` drops it. And `1.0M` and `1.0G` also keep the decimal. So it looks like the `.0` is only stripped when the number is 10 or greater.\n\nThis matches the GNU coreutils `human_readable()` function—it uses one decimal place for values under 10 and no decimals for values 10 and above. I need to adjust my implementation to follow that pattern.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":924,"cache_read_input_tokens":37170,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":924},"output_tokens":31,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"316401c8-9433-40c1-9339-2903c911b953","uuid":"bfc3eb50-a5c1-49d3-aeea-f0ecf17848a3"}
